{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-hadron-half-life-regimes","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:hadron-half-life-regimes","kind":"remark","title":"Half-life regimes and the $q$-landscape","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-hadron-half-life"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-hadron-half-life","tags":["half-life","beta-decay","substrate-parameter","limiting-behaviour","drip-line"],"text":"hadron-half-life-regimes.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"rem:hadron-half-life-regimes\"></a><p>The half-life formula (<a href=\"#prop:hadron-half-life\">hadron half-life</a>) exhibits three regimes:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Regime</th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code> range</th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(q - q^{-1})^5</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(q + q^{-1})</code></th>\n<th>Half-life behaviour</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Classical</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q \\to 1</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\to 0</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\to 2</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">t_{1/2} \\to \\infty</code>: no beta decay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Descartes</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q = q_0</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\approx 3.6 \\times 10^{-4}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\approx 2.012</code></td>\n<td>Observed nuclear lifetimes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deep quantum</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q \\to \\infty</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\to \\infty</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\to \\infty</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">t_{1/2} \\to 0</code>: instantaneous decay</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>-dependent scaling.</strong>\nThe decay rate depends on <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code> through <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(q + q^{-1})(q - q^{-1})^5</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Drip-line asymptotics.</strong>\nAt the drip line, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">|\\delta p| \\gg 1</code> and the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">Q</code>-value grows linearly. The Sargent <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">Q^5</code> scaling predicts extremely short half-lives.</p>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity to <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>.</strong>\nAt <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q = q_0</code>, the logarithmic derivative <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\partial \\ln \\Gamma_\\beta / \\partial \\ln q \\approx 48</code>. A <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1\\%</code> shift in <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code> drives a <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sim 50\\%</code> shift in decay rates. This extreme sensitivity dictates the narrow shape of the valley of stability.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/observations/hadron-half-life-regimes.md","heading":"Remark","number":"16.10.1"}
